  Equilibrium comes out of movement. Movement doesn't come out of equilibrium.
  If the whole cosmos where to reach total equilibrium, movement would become unretrievable.
  Everything would remain dead forever.

  Luckily that's impossible.

  Energy produces movement. Energy changes form but it's indestructible.
  So movement is eternal in both temporal directions.

  Matter is made of various particles, among which: atoms.
  The structure of an atom is similar to the one of the solar system: items gravitate around a bigger item.
  In an atom the planets are called electrons.
  The sun is called the nucleus and is an aggregation of protons and neutrons.
  Let's ignore neutrons.

  Electrons repel electrons. Protons repel protons. Electrons and protons are attracted to each other.
  This tendency is called their electric charge. Assuming a one-dimension space (which is a valid assumption if we also assume
  to charges in isolation), this reciprocal attraction could be represented with two vectors of opposite sign.
  The sign of these vectors is called polarity. Which means: electrons and protons have charges with opposite polarities.
  By convention, electrons get the minus sign and protons get the positive sign.
  Neutrons have a neutral charge (no contribuion to attraction nor repulsion, that's why we're ignoring them).

  The charge of one proton and the charge of one electron have an equal absolute value called elementary charge. Usual symbol: q.
  From where:
    Equilibrium for an atom means equal number of protons and electrons. (Neutral polarity, no urge to move.)
    The atom is then said stable, otherwise it is said unstable.

  An unstable atom has an electric charge. It's called an ion.
  Its protons are at the nucleus, they cannot leave the atom without destroying it.
  So the electric charge has to be due to a lack or an excess of electrons.
  
  A positively charged atom is lacking one or several electrons. It's called a cation.
  A negatively charged atom is having one or several extra electrons. It's called an anion.
  Cations attract electrons. Anions repel electrons.

  If movement happens, its direction depends on polarity: always electrons to cations -- that is negative to positive.

  The mutual attraction of charges with opposite polarities is a force associated with energy -- electrical energy, which is
  whether potential energy (no movement) or a mixture of potential and kinetic energy (movement -- or urge to move + opportunity to move).
  It's never purely kinetic -- this wasn't straightforwardly obvious to me, so let me explain why.
  The mixture comes from energy converting its form: electrons are urged to move (potential) so if they can, they move (kinetic).
  No potential energy means no urge to move, which means no cause for movement, which means no movement.
  This can happen to electrons. But then the energy is no longer electrical. It can nevertheless have become purely kinetic:
  e.g. an ideal motor with no load.

  Notice that a charge in isolation doesn't store energy. You need two groups of charges with opposite polarities before you get
  potential energy. Which means that the energy in one charge is dependant upon the other charges that surround it.
  So the greater the difference in charge between the negatively charged group and the positively charged group, the greater the
  energy per charge in each group.
  The energy per charge in one group is called its electric potential.
  Notice that the urge to move grows with the electric potential.

  Furthermore, let's assume two groups of charges with same polarity but different absolute value.
  The atoms are unstable. If they can reach equilibrium, they will.
  So the electrons will have the urge to move towards the less negative group (even if both are positively charged).
  From where:
    A difference in electric potential is prone to a flow of electrons regardless of polarity.
    (This one's not obvious -- at least to me it wasn't -- meditate it before you move on -- at least I did.)

  We now know what electricity is.

  Oh wait! No we don't...
  Once electrons start moving through matter: this phenomenon is called electricity.


